Looking at this problem, I need to prove that under the two coloring conditions, all elements of $N = \{1, 2, \ldots, n-1\}$ must share the same color.

**Proof.**

Let $c: N \to \{\text{red}, \text{black}\}$ be the coloring. We are given:

**(A)** $c(i) = c(n - i)$ for all $i \in N$.

**(B)** There exists $j \in N$ with $\gcd(j, n) = 1$ such that $c(i) = c(|j - i|)$ for all $i \in N$, $i \neq j$.

Since $j \in N$ and $n - i \in N$ whenever $i \in N$, both conditions are well-defined on $N$.

---

**Step 1: Analyze condition (B) via residues modulo $j$.**

For $i \in N$ with $i \neq j$:

- **If $i > j$:** $|j - i| = i - j$, so $c(i) = c(i - j)$. By iterating, every element of $N$ with residue $r \pmod{j}$ has the same color as the smallest positive representative of that residue class. In particular, all elements sharing a residue class modulo $j$ have the same color.

- **If $i < j$:** $|j - i| = j - i \equiv -i \pmod{j}$, so $c(i) = c(j - i)$. This means residue class $r$ and residue class $-r \pmod{j}$ share the same color.

Combining both cases: the color of any $i \in N$ depends only on the pair $\{r, -r\}$ where $r \equiv i \pmod{j}$. Equivalently, the color classes induced by condition (B) are the orbits of the involution $r \mapsto -r$ on $\mathbb{Z}/j\mathbb{Z}$:
$$\mathcal{C}_r = \{i \in N : i \equiv r \text{ or } i \equiv -r \pmod{j}\}, \quad r \in \mathbb{Z}/j\mathbb{Z},$$
with $\mathcal{C}_r = \mathcal{C}_{-r}$. (The class $\mathcal{C}_0$ consists of all multiples of $j$ in $N$, including $j$ itself, since $c(2j) = c(j)$ when $2j \in N$, etc.)

---

**Step 2: Use condition (A) to merge the classes.**

Condition (A) states $c(i) = c(n - i)$ for all $i \in N$. If $i \equiv r \pmod{j}$, then $n - i \equiv n - r \pmod{j}$. Therefore the class $\mathcal{C}_r$ and the class $\mathcal{C}_{n-r}$ must have the same color. In terms of the involution orbits:
$$\text{color}(\mathcal{C}_r) = \text{color}(\mathcal{C}_{n - r}) \quad \text{for every } r \in \mathbb{Z}/j\mathbb{Z}. \tag{1}$$

Every residue $r \in \mathbb{Z}/j\mathbb{Z}$ is represented in $N$ (since $j \leq n-1$, the set $\{1, \ldots, n-1\}$ covers all residues mod $j$), and for each such $i$, $n - i \in N$ as well, so (1) is valid for all $r$.

---

**Step 3: Combine (A) and (B) to show all classes merge.**

From condition (B), we have $\mathcal{C}_r = \mathcal{C}_{-r}$ (same color). From condition (A), equation (1), $\mathcal{C}_r = \mathcal{C}_{n-r}$ (same color). Applying these in succession:

$$\mathcal{C}_r \;=\; \mathcal{C}_{-r} \;=\; \mathcal{C}_{n - (-r)} \;=\; \mathcal{C}_{n + r} \pmod{j}.$$

Here the first equality uses (B) (class of $-r$ = class of $r$), and the second uses (A) applied to the class of $-r$: $\mathcal{C}_{-r} = \mathcal{C}_{n-(-r)} = \mathcal{C}_{n+r}$.

Therefore:
$$\text{color}(\mathcal{C}_r) = \text{color}(\mathcal{C}_{r + n}) \quad \text{for every } r \in \mathbb{Z}/j\mathbb{Z}. \tag{2}$$

---

**Step 4: Conclude using $\gcd(j, n) = 1$.**

Since $\gcd(j, n) = 1$, the element $n$ is a generator of the additive group $\mathbb{Z}/j\mathbb{Z}$. The relation (2) states that the color is invariant under translation by $n \pmod{j}$. Starting from any residue $r$ and repeatedly adding $n$, we reach every residue class modulo $j$:
$$r, \; r + n, \; r + 2n, \; \ldots \pmod{j}$$
cycles through all of $\mathbb{Z}/j\mathbb{Z}$.

Hence all classes $\mathcal{C}_r$ share the same color, which means every element of $N$ has the same color. $\blacksquare$

